    "id": "cvpr26-69593e5e8543",
    "extraction": {
      "figure": "Figure 3",
      "caption": "Figure 3 : Method overview. (a) Dark3R is trained using paired clean and noisy raw images, ( 𝐈 clean ( 1 ) , 𝐈 clean ( 2 ) ) (\\mathbf{I}_{\\text{clean}}^{({1})},\\mathbf{I}_{\\text{clean}}^{({2})}) and ( 𝐈 noisy ( 1 ) , 𝐈 noisy ( 2 ) ) (\\mathbf{I}_{\\text{noisy}}^{({1})},\\mathbf{I}_{\\text{noisy}}^{({2})}) .\nThe model is initialized from the weights of a pretrained MASt3R [ 33 ] network and adapted to low-light conditions using low-rank adaptation [ 25 ] .\nWe fine-tune the encoder ℰ ~ \\mathcal{\\tilde{E}} , decoder 𝒟 ~ \\mathcal{\\tilde{D}} , and output head ℋ ~ \\mathcal{\\tilde{H}} . We supervise training by minimizing the difference between MASt3R’s encoder features 𝐅 ℰ \\mathbf{F}_{\\mathcal{E}} , decoder features 𝐅 𝒟 \\mathbf{F}_{\\mathcal{D}} , and correspondence map 𝐂 \\mathbf{C} from the clean pair and Dark3R ’s predictions 𝐅 ~ ℰ \\mathbf{\\tilde{F}}_{\\mathcal{E}} , 𝐅 ~ 𝒟 \\mathbf{\\tilde{F}}_{\\mathcal{D}} , and 𝐂 ~ \\mathbf{\\tilde{C}} on the noisy pair. (b) After training, the predicted poses and depth maps from Dark3R enable view synthesis in the dark via a coarse-to-fine optimization process.\nThe rendered novel views are passed through an image signal processor (ISP) to produce the final sRGB outputs.",
